;; si's divergences (doc/scheme/SCHEME-SURVEY.md, "As built: SM5 (write-ahead)", "As built: SCS3"): the classes every non-pass
;; item of bin/si check falls into, each by design, deferred or not applicable, with why and where that was decided. An item is
;; "KIND PROGRAM DETAIL" (arbace.scheme.check). Since SCS3 each program has one judge: the R6RS suites' programs their own report
;; (fails: a test the report says failed, EXPR => RESULT; count: a report of another count of tests than the program's :tests;
;; status: a report and then a non-zero status; no-report: no report, the error's kind), si's own programs and, since SCS6 (b),
;; Larceny's their golden output (crash: another status; error-differs: another kind of error than the golden's :error; differs:
;; stdout differs, its first differing line). SCS6 took out :chez-conditions (Larceny's exceptions.sch, si's R6RS conditions
;; against Chez's &format and &continuation parts): the golden output states si's (§5.3), so it is no item. An item's class is the first whose :match finds it. bin/si divergences lists them. Goi's format
;; (test/goi/divergences.edn). Read with the Clojure reader.
[
 ;; SCS4 (d) (the user, 2026-11-22): (rnrs) gives R6RS's answers, (chezscheme) Chez's, the console two ports for every library;
 ;; :chez-exact-zero and :chez-console-port removed with the records made again on the main host (smalls-1123: racket/base and
 ;; racket/io/ports pass at eval and AOT)
 ;; SCS38 (a) (the user, 2026-11-23): out of the judged cases, n/a: R6RS specifies a condition's types, not its message's text
 {:id :chez-condition-message
  :title "a lexical violation's message is a format string"
  :kind :na
  :why "As Chez's reader, si's raises a lexical violation whose &message is a format control string, \"~? at char ~a of ~s\", its arguments the &irritants (§5.3: si's conditions have Chez's parts); R6RS specifies a condition's types, not its message's text, and Racket's suite expects Racket's own words (\"out of range escape: ...\"). Chez's run fails the same test (bin/si own: chez-fails-too)"
  :decided "§5.3, SCS3, SCS4 (a); SCS38 (a) (the user, 2026-11-23): the case leaves the judged cases, n/a, with the written reason that R6RS specifies a condition's types (&lexical, &message, &irritants), not its message's text, which Racket's suite compares with Racket's own words; alternatives not taken: keep it by design (b), give si's lexical violations Racket's words (c)"
  :match [#"^fails racket/exceptions \(guard \(con \(\(violation\? con\) \(display \(condition-message con\)\) \(quote violation\)\)\) \(read \(open-string-input-port \"\\\\xDDDD;\"\)\)\) => \"~\? at char ~a of ~s\"$"]}
 {:id :suite-ill-formed
  :title "a program of Racket's suite that is ill-formed"
  :kind :na
  :why "Racket's R6RS suite (at its pin) has two run programs that are not programs of the language: run/run.sps imports (tests r6rs run), a library the suite does not have, and run/test.sps calls run-test-tests, which (tests r6rs test) does not export (it exports run-test). R6RS makes both a syntax violation found while the program is expanded, before it runs, and si raises it, as Chez does (bin/si own: chez-errors-too): no test of the program is reached, so the case is none of the eval level's"
  :decided "SCS3"
  :match [#"^no-report racket/run status 255: \[:library-not-found \"\(tests r6rs run\)\"\]$"
          #"^no-report racket/test status 255: \[:unbound-identifier \"run-test-tests\"\]$"]}
]
